            <ArticleTitle>Intestinal pseudo-obstruction caused by neuromyopathy in a patient with systemic sclerosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>912-918</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/s10165-012-0597-x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although gastrointestinal involvement in systemic sclerosis is common, the pathophysiology of small-bowel involvement in systemic sclerosis is not fully understood. We report a case of a 56-year-old woman who has systemic sclerosis with intestinal pseudo-obstruction. The small-bowel manometric findings showed low-amplitude intestinal contraction and absence of the migrating myoelectric complex, and the electrogastrography revealed no increase of the slow-wave power. The manometric and electrogastrographic findings demonstrate that neuromyopathy may play a role in pseudo-obstruction of systemic sclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
